## Current behavior

A controlled `Select.Root` calls `onValueChange(null)` on its own, with `reason: "none"`. It happens when one React update does two things at once:

- removes the item for the current value from the list;
- sets `value` to another item that **is** still in the new list.

Base UI resets the new value to `null`, even though the matching item is rendered.

It reproduces while the popup is open, so the items are mounted. When the same update happens with the popup closed, the value stays correct.

The reset comes from `onMapChange` in `SelectPositioner` (`packages/react/src/select/positioner/SelectPositioner.tsx`):

```ts
if (prevSize !== 0 && !store.state.multiple && value !== null) {
  const selectedValueIndex = findItemIndex(
    store.context.valuesRef.current,
    value,
    isItemEqualToValue,
  );
  if (selectedValueIndex === -1) {
    const initialSelectedValue = store.context.initialValueRef.current;
    const hasInitial =
      initialSelectedValue != null &&
      findItemIndex(store.context.valuesRef.current, initialSelectedValue, isItemEqualToValue) !== -1;
    const nextValue = hasInitial ? initialSelectedValue : null;
    store.context.setValue(nextValue, eventDetails);
    ...
```

The new value (`"bob"` below) is in `store.context.valuesRef.current` after the update, yet `findItemIndex` returns `-1`.

Our reading, which we have not confirmed: `value` inside this `useStableCallback` is the value from the previous commit (`"dave"`). The item-map change fires before the callback sees the new prop. A removed value that is still stale counts as "not found", and the select falls back to `null`.

The same path is on `master` as of 2026-10-03.

## Expected behavior

When the controlled `value` is present among the registered items after the update, the select keeps it and does not call `onValueChange`.

`onMapChange` should only reset a value that is actually missing from the new item set. That means comparing against the value of the current render, not the previous one.

## Reproducible example

```tsx
import * as React from 'react';
import { Select } from '@base-ui/react/select';

export default function App() {
  const [options, setOptions] = React.useState(['alice', 'bob', 'carol', 'dave']);
  const [value, setValue] = React.useState<string | null>('dave');
  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <Select.Root<string>
        value={value}
        onValueChange={(next, details) => {
          console.log('onValueChange', next, details.reason);
          setValue(next);
        }}
        open={open}
        onOpenChange={(next, details) => {
          if (!next && details.reason === 'outside-press') return;
          setOpen(next);
        }}
      >
        <Select.Trigger>
          <Select.Value>{value ?? ''}</Select.Value>
        </Select.Trigger>
        <Select.Portal>
          <Select.Positioner>
            <Select.Popup>
              <Select.List>
                {options.map((option) => (
                  <Select.Item key={option} value={option}>
                    {option}
                  </Select.Item>
                ))}
              </Select.List>
            </Select.Popup>
          </Select.Positioner>
        </Select.Portal>
      </Select.Root>
      <button
        type="button"
        onClick={() => {
          setOptions(['alice', 'bob']);
          setValue('bob');
        }}
      >
        Narrow options and pick bob
      </button>
      <output>{JSON.stringify(value)}</output>
    </div>
  );
}
```

The `onOpenChange` guard only keeps the popup open while the button is clicked.

Steps to reproduce:

1. Open the select. The value is `"dave"`.
2. With the list still open, click **Narrow options and pick bob**.
3. Expected: the value is `"bob"` and nothing is logged. Actual: the console logs `onValueChange null none`, and the value becomes `null`.

## Where we hit it

An audit-log filter in an admin panel. The list of actors is derived from the current rows. When the selected actor dropped out of the rows, the app replaced it with an actor that was still present, in the same update. The filter was cleared instead.

We work around it by ignoring a `null` with reason `"none"` when the controlled value is still among the options.

## Base UI version

1.8.0 (first seen on 1.7.0). Reproduced on 1.8.0 by a test that mounts the example above in jsdom with Testing Library: after the click the value is `null`.

## Which browser are you using?

Chrome (current stable)

## Which OS are you using?

macOS
